/-!
# The pseudo-coherence of an object of `D(O)` with a left dual

Let `(C, O)` be a ringed site and let `M` be an object of `D(O)` with a left dual `N` in the
symmetric monoidal category `D(O)` of Stacks 0FPT. The proof of Stacks 0FPV shows that `M` is
pseudo-coherent once it is bounded above, by descending induction: if `M` is `m`-pseudo-coherent,
it is `(m - 1)`-pseudo-coherent.

The step is local. Over an object of `C` carrying an approximation `α : E ⟶ M` in degree `m` by
a strictly perfect complex concentrated in degrees `≥ m`, the cone of `α` is concentrated in
degrees `≤ m - 1` and is represented by a complex `𝓛` of flat modules, so `M` is the cone of a
morphism `𝓛⟦-1⟧ ⟶ E`: a K-flat complex `M^•` which agrees with `E` above `m - 1`. The source's
argument then makes `M` locally a retract of the subcomplex `K ⊂ M^•` generated by the image of
`E` and by finitely many sections; `K` agrees with `M^•` above `m - 1`, and its term in degree
`m - 1` is a quotient of a finite free module, so `K` is `(m - 1)`-pseudo-coherent, and so is its
direct summand.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_coneRepresentative`: an object approximated in degree `m`
  by a complex concentrated in degrees `≥ m` is the cone of a morphism out of the shift of a
  complex of flat modules concentrated in degrees `≤ m - 1`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_Q_of_tail`: a complex which agrees above `k`
  with a strictly perfect complex and whose term in degree `k` is a quotient of a direct summand
  of a finite free module is `k`-pseudo-coherent.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_restriction_of_sliceRetract`: the step on
  one member of the covering of `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_presieve_sliceRetract`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_sub_one_of_isPseudoCoherentFrom`: an
  `m`-pseudo-coherent object with a left dual is `(m - 1)`-pseudo-coherent.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherent_of_exactPairing_of_isLE`: a bounded above object
  with a left dual is pseudo-coherent.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FPV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FPV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FPT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FPT)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08FT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08FT)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08FU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08FU)
-/

